m68k: split a move whose auto-modified source register the destination uses.

At -O2, gcc 15.2's late-combine pass substitutes one address into
another and can make

	move.b (%a0)+,(0,%a0,%d1.l)

from a copy loop whose destination is computed from its source. In RTL
that insn is well defined: every use of %a0 in it sees the value before
the increment. The 68000 family does not execute it that way. It
finishes the source operand, post-increment or pre-decrement included,
before it calculates the destination address, so the destination is off
by the size of the move -- and QEMU, which follows the hardware, does
the same. A copy lands a byte (or two, or four) away from where it was
meant to, with nothing to say so.

It was found in the Sage040 dynamic linker, where a thread's TLS image
arrived shifted by one byte and its last byte landed on the next thing
in memory. Reproduced from four lines of C:

	struct m { unsigned long a, b, c; const void *img; };
	void cp3(unsigned long base, const struct m *m)
	{
	    unsigned char *dp = (unsigned char *)(base + m->a);
	    const unsigned char *sp = m->img;
	    unsigned long n = m->c;
	    while (n--) *dp++ = *sp++;
	}

The fix is at output, where every such insn has to pass: a memory to
memory move whose source address is (An)+ or -(An), with An in the
destination address, is written as the plain move followed by the
adjustment of An. addq/subq to an address register leave the condition
codes alone, so the flags still describe the move; the recorded flag
operands are cleared, because once An has moved neither operand's
address names the location they describe. The destination-side case
needs nothing: the hardware computes the source first, which is what
RTL means.

